 Here's a summary of what the regex does:\n\n## Regex Pattern Overview\n\nThe pattern saved in `/app/regex.txt` correctly:\n\n1. **Matches dates only on IPv4-containing lines**: Uses a positive lookahead `(?=.*IPv4)` to ensure the line contains a valid IPv4 address before attempting to match any dates\n\n2. **Captures only the last date**: Uses `.*?` (non-greedy matching) combined with a negative lookahead `(?!.*DATE)` to ensure no more valid dates appear after the captured one\n\n3. **Validates date formats**:\n   - **31-day months** (Jan, Mar, May, Jul, Aug, Oct, Dec): days 01-31\n   - **30-day months** (Apr, Jun, Sep, Nov): days 01-30  \n   - **February**: days 01-29 (without distinguishing leap years)\n\n4. **Validates IPv4 addresses**:\n   - Each octet: 0-255\n   - No leading zeros (except \"0\" itself)\n\n5. **Enforces word boundaries**:\n   - Uses negative lookbehind/lookahead `(?<![a-zA-Z0-9])` and `(?![a-zA-Z0-9])`\n   - Prevents false matches like \"user1134-12-1234\" or dates immediately adjacent to other alphanumeric characters\n\n6. **Works with `re.MULTILINE`**: The `^` and `$` anchors correctly match line boundaries\n\n**All test cases passed**, including edge cases like February 29, invalid days for specific months, and multi-line log files.","ttft_ms":161917,"type":"result","duration_ms":215525,"uuid":"0a8f8bd0-63ab-4739-a1c4-e9b9a494652f","ttft_stream_ms":882,"time_to_request_ms":44,"queued_turn_count":0}
